-- allium: 3
-- tasks.allium
--
-- Scope: Task lifecycle — creation, status movement, reorder, archive, copy, editor,
--        and the board-wide multi-selection the batch rules consume.
-- Excludes: Agent health and hooks (see agent-health.allium);
--           dispatch and retry (see dispatch.allium);
--           PR/wrap-up (see pr-workflow.allium);
--           split pane (see split-pane.allium);
--           MCP task tools (see mcp-task-tools.allium);
--           epic lifecycle (see epics.allium);
--           domain model (see core.allium).

use "./core.allium" as core

-- The one board-wide multi-selection every batch rule in this file
-- consumes. See "Multi-Selection".
given {
    selection: core/BoardSelection
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Task Creation ==

rule CreateTask {
    when: CreateTask(title, description, repo_path, plan?, epic?, tag?, sort_order?, base_branch?, wrap_up_mode?, auto_run_plan?, schedule_interval_secs?, pinned_branch?)

    ensures: core/Task.created(
        title: title,
        description: description,
        repo_path: repo_path,
        status: backlog,
        sub_status: core/SubStatus.default_for(backlog),
        plan_path: plan,
        epic: epic,
        tag: tag,
        sort_order: sort_order,
        base_branch: base_branch ?? core/config.default_branch,
        wrap_up_mode: wrap_up_mode,
        auto_run_plan: auto_run_plan ?? false,
        schedule_interval_secs: schedule_interval_secs,
        pinned_branch: pinned_branch,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- tag is chosen in the TUI creation form's tag-picker step, a
        -- single-keypress picker. The accepted keys are: b -> Bug,
        -- f -> Feature, c -> Chore, p -> PrReview, r -> Research,
        -- x -> Fix. Enter means no tag; Esc cancels creation. Any other
        -- key is silently ignored and the picker stays open. Note the
        -- picker's on-screen hint text currently advertises a different,
        -- smaller key set than the handler accepts.
        -- base_branch is set from user input during TUI task creation
        -- (InputBaseBranch step). That step renders the BaseBranchPicker
        -- surface (see dispatch.allium): a fuzzy picker over the branches
        -- previously used for the selected repo, prefilled with that repo's
        -- most-recently-used branch and falling back to config.default_branch
        -- ("main") when the repo has no history yet. Finishing the manual form
        -- records the chosen branch into the repo's history by emitting
        -- BaseBranchUsed(repo_path, base_branch) (see dispatch.allium:
        -- RecordBaseBranch). Only this manual form records history —
        -- quick-dispatch (CreateQuickTask) and the MCP create_task tool do not.
        -- If omitted (e.g. quick-dispatch), base_branch
        -- defaults to config.default_branch (usually "main").
        -- wrap_up_mode is set during the TUI creation form (InputWrapUpMode
        -- step, after InputBaseBranch) or via the MCP create_task tool.
        -- When null, the wrap-up path is decided at wrap-up time.
        --
        -- == The schedule step (TUI creation form tail) ==
        -- schedule_interval_secs and pinned_branch are set from the TUI
        -- creation form's LAST step, after InputWrapUpMode, or via the MCP
        -- create_task tool. Both default to null: a task nobody schedules is
        -- indistinguishable from every task created before these fields
        -- existed.
        --
        -- The step is GATED rather than unconditional. After the wrap-up
        -- picker the form shows a single-keypress gate
        -- ("Schedule: [Enter] none  [s] configure"), which is the same
        -- single-key-picker shape as the tag and wrap-up steps:
        --   - Enter (or any unrecognised key falling through to Enter's
        --     meaning is NOT the rule -- an unrecognised key is ignored and
        --     the gate stays open, exactly as the tag picker behaves) creates
        --     the task immediately with both fields null.
        --   - "s" opens two free-text steps in order: the interval, then the
        --     pinned branch. Only after the second does the task get created.
        --   - Esc cancels creation outright, like every other form step.
        -- The gate exists because scheduling is rare and creation is
        -- frequent: two unconditional text steps would tax every task created
        -- with two extra keypresses for a field almost none of them set. The
        -- gate costs one keypress instead, and only the scheduling case pays
        -- for the rest.
        --
        -- Both text steps render the shared TextInputField surface
        -- (dispatch.allium), not a picker: neither field has a candidate list
        -- to fuzzy-match against, so every printable character is literal
        -- text and no key is a select shortcut. Enter on an empty buffer
        -- means "leave this one null" and advances, so a user who takes the
        -- "s" branch may still set only one of the two.
        --
        -- The interval is typed using the shared interval-literal grammar
        -- (core.allium: "How a human types an interval") -- bare seconds or a
        -- s/m/h/d suffix, strictly positive. Input that does not parse is
        -- REJECTED IN PLACE: an error is shown and the step stays open with
        -- the buffer intact, rather than being coerced or silently dropped.
        -- This is the one form step that can refuse to advance on non-empty
        -- input, and it refuses rather than accepting garbage because the
        -- value drives a background loop the user will not be watching.
        --
        -- pinned_branch takes the branch name literally, with no validation
        -- that the branch exists. The check cannot be usefully done here (the
        -- branch may be created before the first scheduled run) and its
        -- absence is caught at dispatch time -- see dispatch.allium:
        -- PinnedWorktreeBranchIsNeverDerived.
        -- auto_run_plan defaults to false. When true, a dispatched agent
        -- with a plan skips the "summarize and ask permission" step (see
        -- dispatch.allium). Set by decompose-review's create_task calls,
        -- since those plans already went through the work-package
        -- confirmation step; not exposed in the TUI creation form.
        -- When invoked from the epic view, epic is set to the
        -- current epic, auto-linking the new task as a subtask.
        -- When epic is a grouped root (group_by_repo = true), the task is
        -- routed to the matching per-repo sub-epic via route_target, using
        -- task.repo_path as the routing key.
}

rule SetWrapUpMode {
    when: SetWrapUpMode(task, mode?)

    ensures: task.wrap_up_mode = mode
    ensures: task.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Sets or clears the preferred wrap-up path on an existing task.
        -- Exposed via MCP update_task(wrap_up_mode: "rebase"|"pr"|"done"|null)
        -- and the task editor (WRAP_UP_MODE section).
        -- mode = null clears the preference; the next wrap-up will prompt
        -- the user to choose.
        -- This rule deliberately has no status precondition — the preference
        -- can be updated at any point in the task lifecycle.
}

-- Quick dispatch is gated on repo trust before anything is created. The
-- gate exists for the same reason DispatchTask's does (see dispatch.allium,
-- rule DispatchTask): a fresh worktree launched into a repo Claude Code does
-- not trust stalls on Claude Code's own interactive trust prompt, which
-- silently defeats quick dispatch's unattended, immediate contract.

rule QuickDispatchTrustGate {
    when: QuickDispatch(repo_path, epic_id?)

    ensures:
        if is_repo_trusted(repo_path):
            QuickDispatchTrusted(repo_path: repo_path, epic_id: epic_id)
        else:
            ConfirmTrustRepoQuickDispatch(repo_path: repo_path, epic_id: epic_id)

    @guidance
        -- is_repo_trusted(repo_path) is the same black-box trust check
        -- DispatchTask uses; it reads Claude Code's own per-project trust
        -- record, keyed on the REPO path (not the worktree path). Trust on
        -- the repo path is what makes the worktree under
        -- <repo_path>/.worktrees/... launchable, the same granularity the
        -- DispatchTask gate relies on.
        -- The check runs before any Task is created: on the untrusted
        -- branch nothing is persisted, only a confirmation prompt is
        -- raised ("Repo '<path>' not trusted by Claude Code — trust
        -- it? [y/N]"). The pending creation parameters are held in the
        -- prompt itself (there is no task id to key on yet), which is the
        -- one structural difference from DispatchTask's gate.
        -- Declining (any key other than y/Y) dismisses the prompt and
        -- creates nothing — same cancel semantics as DispatchTask's trust
        -- prompt.
        -- If the trust check itself errors (unreadable trust record), the
        -- error is surfaced through the status bar and neither branch is
        -- taken: no prompt, no task.
}

rule TrustRepoForQuickDispatch {
    when: TrustAndQuickDispatch(repo_path, epic_id?)

    ensures: RepoTrustGranted(repo_path: repo_path)
    ensures: QuickDispatchTrusted(repo_path: repo_path, epic_id: epic_id)

    @guidance
        -- Accepting the ConfirmTrustRepoQuickDispatch prompt (y/Y).
        -- Trust is granted on repo_path, after which the held creation
        -- parameters proceed exactly as the trusted branch of
        -- QuickDispatchTrustGate would have.
        -- RepoTrustGranted makes is_repo_trusted(repo_path) true for
        -- every later dispatch into that repo, quick or standard — the
        -- two entry points share one trust record.
        -- If granting trust fails (the trust record cannot be written),
        -- the failure is surfaced as an error and no task is created.
}

rule CreateQuickTask {
    when: QuickDispatchTrusted(repo_path, epic_id?)

    ensures:
        let task = core/Task.created(
            title: "Quick task",
            description: "",
            repo_path: repo_path,
            status: backlog,
            sub_status: core/SubStatus.default_for(backlog),
            epic: if epic_id != null: core/Epic{id: epic_id} else: null,
            base_branch: detect_default_branch(repo_path),
            created_at: now,
            updated_at: now
        )
        AgentLaunched(task: task, mode: quick)

    @guidance
        -- Reached only through QuickDispatchTrustGate, i.e. once the repo
        -- is trusted — either it already was, or the user just granted
        -- trust via TrustRepoForQuickDispatch. Nothing is created on the
        -- untrusted path.
        -- The originating stimulus is the Shift+D (capital D) TUI key,
        -- which emits QuickDispatch. When exactly
        -- one saved repo path exists, it is used directly. Otherwise
        -- (zero or multiple saved paths), a picker
        -- (InputMode::QuickDispatch) is shown, which renders the shared
        -- RepoPathPicker surface (see below). With zero saved paths the
        -- picker opens to an empty list; the user types a new path and
        -- presses Enter to submit it as a NewPath entry.
        -- Per the picker's NoPrintableShortcut guarantee, j/k and
        -- digits filter; they do not navigate or select.
        -- When invoked from the epic view, epic_id is set to the
        -- current epic, linking the quick task as a subtask.
        -- The task title defaults to "Quick task"; the agent is expected
        -- to rename the task via update_task once the user describes the
        -- goal. The quick-dispatch agent uses a dedicated prompt that
        -- asks the user what they want to achieve, then instructs the
        -- agent to write a plan in docs/plans/ and attach it.
        -- base_branch is resolved from `git symbolic-ref
        -- refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` against repo_path, falling back to
        -- "main" when origin/HEAD is unavailable. This prevents
        -- dispatch failures on repos whose default branch is not main.
        -- schedule_interval_secs and pinned_branch are always null here.
        -- Quick dispatch runs no form, so it never reaches CreateTask's
        -- schedule gate — and an immediate one-shot agent is the opposite of
        -- a recurring one. A quick task that should recur is scheduled after
        -- the fact, through the task editor or update_task.
        -- AgentLaunched fires immediately after creation: the task goes
        -- Backlog -> Running with a worktree and tmux window provisioned,
        -- and — since QuickDispatchTrustGate — behind the same repo-trust
        -- gate DispatchTask applies. It is still NOT the DispatchTask rule:
        -- quick dispatch has no backlog/tag preconditions (the task it just
        -- created is trivially backlog and untagged), no base-branch history
        -- recording (see CreateTask guidance), and its own prompt variant
        -- (build_quick_dispatch_prompt, mode: quick). Trust is the one gate
        -- the two entry points share.
}

-- == Status Movement ==

rule MoveTaskForward {
    when: MoveTask(task, forward)

    requires: task.status != done
    requires: task.status != archived

    let next = next_status(task.status)
    let was_running = task.status = running

    -- Moving to Done requires explicit confirmation via ConfirmDone
    requires: next != done

    ensures: task.status = next
    ensures: task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(next)
    ensures: task.updated_at = now
    ensures:
        if was_running:
            task.stop_pending = false

    @guidance
        -- Manual movement (forward or backward) clears the task's agent
        -- notification/poll tracking flags: the review-notified and
        -- needs-input-notified markers, the last PR-poll timestamp, and the
        -- card message flash. Nothing else is cleared — there is no
        -- separate tmux-output or crash-detection state.
        -- A move out of running also voids any deferred Stop
        -- (task.stop_pending), because the bit belongs to the turn the task
        -- was running under. was_running reads the pre-move status; an
        -- ensures-block `if` would read the post-move one. See
        -- core/Task's PendingStopOnlyWhileRunning invariant for why every
        -- leaving-running write must do this.
        -- sub_status is always reset to the default for the new status.
        -- Any manual-move transition into Running (not just Review ->
        -- Running) also stamps last_pre_tool_use_at = now, so
        -- ClassifyAgentActivity does not immediately report the task Stale
        -- (see agent-health.allium). This includes Backlog -> Running, which
        -- provisions no worktree; the seed is harmless there because both
        -- the classifier and crash detection only consider tasks with a
        -- tmux_window.
        -- Supports batch operation on multi-selected tasks. Batch forward
        -- splits when the selection includes Review tasks: non-Review
        -- tasks move immediately, Review tasks are collected for a single
        -- batch ConfirmDone prompt.
}

rule MoveTaskBackward {
    when: MoveTask(task, backward)

    requires: task.status != backlog
    requires: task.status != archived

    let prev = prev_status(task.status)
    let was_running = task.status = running

    -- Kill tmux window when moving backward, but preserve worktree for resume
    ensures: task.status = prev
    ensures: task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(prev)
    ensures: task.tmux_window = null
    ensures: task.updated_at = now
    ensures:
        if prev = review:
            task.sort_order = null
    ensures:
        if was_running:
            task.stop_pending = false

    @guidance
        -- Manual movement (forward or backward) clears the task's agent
        -- notification/poll tracking flags: the review-notified and
        -- needs-input-notified markers, the last PR-poll timestamp, and the
        -- card message flash. Nothing else is cleared — there is no
        -- separate tmux-output or crash-detection state.
        -- A move out of running also voids any deferred Stop — see
        -- MoveTaskForward's guidance and core/Task's
        -- PendingStopOnlyWhileRunning invariant.
        -- sub_status is always reset to the default for the new status.
        -- The worktree is preserved so the task can be resumed later
        -- via ResumeTask.
        -- Supports batch operation on multi-selected tasks.
        -- prev = review is only reached when the task's prior status was
        -- done (the sole backward edge into review), so this is the
        -- leaving-done case: task.sort_order is cleared back to null,
        -- mirroring ConfirmDone's entering-done set.
}

rule ConfirmDone {
    when: ConfirmDone(task)

    requires: task.status != done
    requires: task.status != archived

    let was_running = task.status = running

    ensures: task.status = done
    ensures: task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(done)
    ensures: task.tmux_window = null
    ensures: task.updated_at = now
    ensures: task.sort_order = completion_recency_rank(now)
    ensures:
        if was_running:
            task.stop_pending = false

    @guidance
        -- Confirmation prompt for the transition into Done. Used by
        -- single-task forward moves, BatchMoveForward, and the 'x' key.
        -- Each task is individually guarded before transitioning: tasks
        -- already in Done or Archived are skipped rather than re-moved.
        -- The tmux window is killed but the worktree is preserved —
        -- unlike ArchiveTask, which removes it. As with MoveTaskForward,
        -- the task's agent notification/poll tracking flags are cleared,
        -- and a deferred Stop is voided when the task was running.
        --
        -- Reached from forward movement only when the next status is Done
        -- (i.e. from Review). The 'x' key reaches it from *any* non-Done
        -- status, moving the task straight to Done rather than one step
        -- forward — see the ArchiveKeyRouting guidance on ArchiveTask.
        --
        -- completion_recency_rank(now) is a black-box helper: it returns a
        -- value such that more-recently-completed tasks sort first among
        -- Done items (implementation detail: the negated current
        -- timestamp in milliseconds, so lower Integer values are more
        -- recent). The identical mechanism sets task.sort_order at every
        -- other transition into done: PrMerged, PrClosed, and
        -- ExitSession's non-pr branch (all in
        -- pr-workflow.allium), and epics.allium's MoveEpicForward when
        -- entering done. Leaving done (MoveTaskBackward, ArchiveTask when
        -- the task was done) clears task.sort_order back to null.
}

-- == Multi-Selection ==

-- Two different things are called "select all", and conflating them is the
-- trap this subsection exists to close.
--
--   1. The select-all *cursor position* (core.allium's on_select_all): the
--      cursor is parked on a column's header rather than on a card. Row
--      navigation sets it — moving up off the first row lands there — and
--      moving back down leaves it. It is a place the cursor can be, not a
--      selection, and moving off it changes no selection at all.
--   2. The select-all *action* (SelectAllColumn below): it mutates the
--      selection and does not touch the cursor position. It can be invoked
--      while the cursor is on the header or on any card in the column.
--
-- They are independent. Only ClearSelection touches both.

rule SelectAllColumn {
    -- tasks and epics are the column's currently *visible* selectable
    -- items, in the sense fixed by the guidance below. is_edge_column says
    -- whether the cursor column is one of the two edge columns.
    when: SelectAllColumn(is_edge_column, tasks, epics)

    -- Edge columns cannot select-all. This is the behavioural counterpart
    -- of the rendering rule that hides the checkbox on them
    -- (core.allium: ColumnChrome.shows_select_all_checkbox) — the two must
    -- move together, so neither should be changed alone.
    requires: not is_edge_column

    -- An empty column is a no-op.
    requires: tasks.count > 0 or epics.count > 0

    ensures:
        if tasks.all(t => t in selection.selected_tasks)
            and epics.all(e => e in selection.selected_epics):
            for t in tasks:
                selection.selected_tasks.remove(t)
            for e in epics:
                selection.selected_epics.remove(e)
        else:
            for t in tasks:
                selection.selected_tasks.add(t)
            for e in epics:
                selection.selected_epics.add(e)

    @guidance
        -- Bound to `a` in any task-status column, and to Enter while the
        -- cursor is on that column's header. Enter on the header toggles
        -- exactly as `a` does — it is not a clear.
        --
        -- What gets selected is what is *visible*. The column's items are
        -- the ones the board is currently showing there, so the active epic
        -- scope, the repo filter and the search query all apply (see
        -- core.allium: tasks_for_current_view and the board search filter).
        -- A task that shares the column's status but is filtered out is not
        -- reachable by this action. This is the safety-relevant part: batch
        -- operations consume the resulting selection, so "select all" means
        -- all of what you can see, never all of what has this status.
        --
        -- Scope is one column, the focused one, never the whole board. The
        -- selection it feeds is board-wide though, so selecting all in
        -- Backlog and then all in Running leaves both columns' items
        -- selected. Per-column action, board-wide result.
        --
        -- Covers tasks and epics together. The decorative items in a column
        -- — epic header rules, sub-status labels, the orphan separator —
        -- are not selectable and are skipped.
        --
        -- The toggle is deliberately asymmetric, and this is the part worth
        -- knowing: it completes to full rather than inverting. From a
        -- partial selection it selects everything; only when every task and
        -- epic in the column is already selected does it deselect. So two
        -- presses from a partial state leave the column empty, not back at
        -- the partial set the user started from — the intermediate state is
        -- not recoverable by pressing again.
        --
        -- An empty column is a no-op: nothing is selected, deselected, or
        -- otherwise changed.
        --
        -- Ordering of batch operations over the resulting selection is not
        -- specified here — see the Batch Status Movement and BatchArchive
        -- rules for what consumes it.
}

rule ClearSelection {
    when: ClearSelection()

    ensures:
        selection.selected_tasks = {}
        selection.selected_epics = {}

    @guidance
        -- The only operation that touches both meanings of "select all":
        -- it empties the selection sets *and* releases the select-all cursor
        -- position (core.allium's on_select_all, which no entity in these
        -- specs carries as a field — it is board view state, described in
        -- core.allium under Cursor Selection Preservation). Every other path
        -- affects one or the other, never both.
}

-- == Batch Status Movement ==

rule BatchMoveForward {
    when: BatchMoveTasks(tasks, forward)

    -- Split: non-Review tasks move immediately via MoveTaskForward.
    -- Review tasks are collected and confirmed together via ConfirmDone.
    let non_review = tasks where status != review
    let review_tasks = tasks where status = review

    ensures:
        for task in non_review:
            MoveTaskForward(task)
    ensures:
        if review_tasks.count > 0:
            ConfirmDone(review_tasks)

    @guidance
        -- When all selected tasks are non-Review, they move forward
        -- without any confirmation prompt. When some are in Review,
        -- the non-Review tasks still move immediately while the Review
        -- tasks are held for a single batch confirmation. This avoids
        -- individual prompts per Review task.
}

rule BatchMoveBackward {
    when: BatchMoveTasks(tasks, backward)

    ensures:
        for task in tasks:
            MoveTaskBackward(task)
}

-- == Reorder ==

rule ReorderItem {
    when: ReorderItem(item, direction)
    -- item: Task or Epic in the current visual column
    -- direction: +1 (down) or -1 (up)

    let items = column_items(item)
    let current_index = index_of(items, item)
    let target_index = current_index + direction

    requires: target_index >= 0
    requires: target_index < items.count

    let target = element_at(items, target_index)
    let item_eff = item.sort_order ?? item.id
    let target_eff = target.sort_order ?? target.id

    ensures:
        if item_eff = target_eff:
            -- Equal effective values: offset the moved item by 1
            item.sort_order = item_eff + direction
            target.sort_order = target_eff
        else:
            -- Swap effective sort_order values
            item.sort_order = target_eff
            target.sort_order = item_eff

    @guidance
        -- Operates on both tasks and epics within the same visual
        -- column. The effective sort_order is sort_order ?? id
        -- (falling back to the item's database id when unset).
        -- Both updated sort_order values are persisted to the database.
        -- No-op when the item is already at the boundary of its column.
}

-- == Move To Epic ==

rule MoveTaskToEpic {
    when: MoveTaskToEpic(task, new_epic?)

    requires: task.status != archived
    -- When a target epic is chosen it must exist. A null new_epic
    -- detaches the task into a standalone (board-level) task.
    requires:
        if new_epic != null: core/Epic.exists(new_epic)

    ensures: task.epic = route_target(new_epic, task.repo_path)
    ensures: task.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Triggered from the kanban board or an epic view by pressing `m`
        -- on a task card (when `m` is pressed on an epic card it runs the
        -- epic ReparentEpic flow instead — see epics.allium). Operates on
        -- the single selected, non-archived task.
        --
        -- A collapsible tree overlay lists only epics that are relevant
        -- targets: it excludes epics in Done or Archived status and epics
        -- hidden by the active board filter (repo include/exclude and the
        -- only-active filter). The board search query is deliberately NOT
        -- applied here — this picker has its own input buffer. See
        -- board_search_filter in core.allium. Unlike epic reparenting there is
        -- no cycle
        -- concern, so no descendant exclusion applies; every visible,
        -- active epic is a valid target. When status filtering removes a
        -- parent but keeps an eligible child, the child is re-rooted to the
        -- top level so it stays selectable. The first item is always
        -- "— no parent —", which sets epic to null.
        -- Tree navigation: j/k = up/down, l/Space/→ = expand,
        -- h/← = collapse, Enter = select.
        -- A [y/n] confirmation prompt is shown before committing the
        -- change. Pressing n returns to the picker; Esc/q cancels entirely.
        -- The confirmation is UI sugar — the normative outcome is the
        -- move itself, after the user has confirmed.
        --
        -- route_target(epic, repo_path): when new_epic.group_by_repo = true,
        -- route_target redirects the task to the matching per-repo sub-epic
        -- (creating it if needed). When new_epic is a specific sub-epic (not
        -- a grouped root), or when new_epic.group_by_repo = false, the task
        -- is assigned directly to new_epic. When new_epic is null,
        -- route_target returns null (no redirection; the task becomes
        -- standalone).
        --
        -- repo_path-change reroute: when a task's repo_path is changed and
        -- it belongs to a grouped root, the service also calls
        -- reroute_on_repo_change to move it to the correct sub-epic for
        -- its new repo_path. This happens transparently on any update that
        -- changes repo_path when the task is under a grouped parent.
        --
        -- Moving a task changes which epic(s) own it: the previous epic
        -- (if any) and the new epic (if any) both have their status
        -- recalculated per the EpicStatusRecalculation invariant in
        -- epics.allium. updated_at is refreshed automatically by the
        -- database layer; the assertion above documents the intent.
        --
        -- Feed-sourced tasks (external_id set) may be re-ingested under
        -- their original feed epic on the next poll; moving such a task is
        -- permitted but not durable against the feed.
}

-- == Archive ==

-- TaskTeardown (conceptual): the single named account of what it means to
-- release a task's live resources. It is NOT a new behaviour — it is the
-- behaviour ArchiveTask and DeleteTask already specify, stated once here so
-- that the rules below, and the feed removal clauses in feeds.allium, can
-- reference it by name instead of restating it.
--
-- TaskTeardown(task) means, in order:
--
--   1. Kill the task's tmux window if it has one.
--   2. Remove the task's git worktree if it has one.
--   3. Best-effort branch deletion. A failure here is not fatal to the
--      operation that requested the teardown.
--
-- A task whose worktree or window has already been removed out of band tears
-- down cleanly: step 2 treats an already-absent worktree as released.
--
-- TeardownIsOwedWheneverThereIsSomethingToRelease. The teardown is performed
-- when the task owns a worktree OR a tmux window; only a task owning NEITHER
-- runs no commands at all. The two columns are independent, so all four
-- combinations are reachable, and each step is owed exactly on its own
-- condition:
--
--   * worktree and window -> steps 1, 2, 3.
--   * window, no worktree -> step 1 only. There is no branch to delete either,
--     because a task that never had a worktree never had a branch.
--   * worktree, no window -> steps 2 and 3.
--   * neither -> nothing.
--
-- Step 1 is NOT contingent on step 2 having something to remove. Making the
-- whole teardown conditional on the worktree leaks the window of a window-only
-- task, and it is what the archive/delete path did until #4096 while the feed
-- path did not — the divergence that motivated collapsing both onto one
-- primitive, src/dispatch/worktree.rs::teardown_task, which takes the worktree
-- and the window as independent optionals and is now the only implementation of
-- this clause.
--
-- WorktreeReleaseIsGated. Step 2 either releases the worktree or it does not,
-- and the difference is observable. A FAILED removal:
--
--   a. is reported to the operator, and
--   b. is recorded durably in the application log at ERROR, naming the task and
--      the worktree path, and
--   c. does NOT let the requesting operation forget the path. ArchiveTask keeps
--      the worktree pointer on the task, and DeleteTask does not remove the row
--      at all (see both rules below).
--
-- (c) is what makes the failure recoverable rather than an orphan: the task
-- still names the directory and the branch that are still on disk, so the
-- operator can retry the teardown by deleting the archived task again. Without
-- it, a transient git failure permanently strands a worktree that nothing in
-- the system references — the mechanism investigated in
-- docs/plans/archive/2026-08-11-3897-worktree-cleanup-investigation.md.
--
-- Two paths are deliberately exempt from (c), and both still owe (a) and (b):
-- RetryFresh (its own postcondition clears the pointer, and the immediate
-- re-dispatch derives the same worktree path, so a retained pointer would
-- change nothing observable), and DeleteEpic in epics.allium (it removes the
-- subtask rows in one operation, leaving nothing that could hold a pointer).
--
-- The gate is keyed on step 2, and ONLY on step 2. A task with no worktree has
-- nothing to release: ArchiveTask and DeleteTask both bind released from
-- worktree_removed, which is vacuously true there, so the requesting operation's
-- follow-up — the pointer clear, or the row delete — is applied even if step 1
-- FAILED. Such a failure is warn-logged and not surfaced. It must not withhold
-- the follow-up: there is no directory left on disk for a retained pointer to
-- make reachable, so withholding would gate a row delete on a tmux call and
-- strand the row instead of the resource. Only the worktree arm can produce the
-- reported-and-ERROR-logged failure of (a) and (b).
--

-- Referenced by: ArchiveTask and DeleteTask below (whose own ensures clauses
-- remain the normative statement of their postconditions), and by the feed
-- removal clauses in feeds.allium (RoleRoutedFeedSync, GroupedFeedUpsert,
-- FlatFeedReconcile, UpsertFeedTasks), where removing a stale feed task must
-- perform this same teardown rather than dropping the row and orphaning the
-- worktree of an in-flight agent.
--
-- Implementation: src/dispatch/worktree.rs::teardown_task, the one primitive
-- that performs all three steps. Its two callers are policy wrappers over it and
-- differ only in what they do with a failure: src/runtime/tasks.rs::exec_cleanup
-- gates the follow-up (see below), and src/feed/mod.rs::cleanup_removed_feed_tasks
-- warn-logs. Neither may branch on the worktree itself — that branch lives in the
-- primitive, per TeardownIsOwedWheneverThereIsSomethingToRelease.
--
-- Step 2's outcome is reported, not inferred: teardown_task fails with
-- src/dispatch/worktree.rs::TeardownFailure, whose worktree_left names the
-- worktree still on disk (None when there was none to release). exec_cleanup
-- matches on that field rather than on the arguments it passed, so the gate does
-- not silently depend on the order the primitive happens to run its steps in.
--
-- WorktreeReleaseIsGated is implemented by the follow-up that travels with the
-- teardown: src/tui/commands/task.rs::CleanupFollowUp, chosen at the request
-- site (src/tui/mod.rs::take_cleanup callers) and applied only on the removal's
-- completion path, which reports CleanupSucceeded or CleanupFailed
-- (src/tui/messages/task.rs) from src/runtime/tasks.rs::exec_cleanup. The
-- ERROR record is the tracing::error! there; the two handlers are
-- src/tui/update/lifecycle.rs::handle_cleanup_succeeded and
-- handle_cleanup_failed. The teardown runs detached because it shells out to
-- git, which is why the follow-up cannot simply be sequenced beside it.
--
-- WorktreeIsNeverShared. Clause 2 is UNCONDITIONAL, and no path needs to ask
-- whether another task also holds the worktree, because no two tasks can. A
-- worktree path embeds the task id: src/dispatch/worktree.rs::provision_worktree
-- derives it as <repo>/.worktrees/<task-id>-<slug>, so two distinct tasks can
-- never name the same path, and its reuse branch reuses a directory only for
-- that same id-scoped path, i.e. for the same task. The id prefix, not the
-- slug, is what guarantees this: a rename never recomputes the stored path, and
-- a later re-provision after a rename yields a second path under the SAME id
-- rather than a collision with a different task's.
--
-- The worktree column has exactly one writer, patch_task in
-- src/db/queries/tasks.rs, and every value that SETS it originates from
-- provision_worktree — reaching the column through
-- src/mcp/handlers/tasks/dispatch.rs or src/runtime/tasks.rs::exec_persist_task.
-- No other surface can introduce one: CopyTask copies into a draft that has no
-- worktree field, the MCP update_task schema does not expose worktree, the feed
-- upsert omits the column, and no CLI subcommand writes it. A hand-edited
-- database could fabricate two rows on one path; that is not a supported input.
--
-- Do NOT add a shared-worktree check on any path. Clause 2 once carried such an
-- exception; it was removed because the condition is unsatisfiable, and the
-- branch it gated had silently contradicted clause 1 for as long as it existed.
--
-- Implementation of the feed path: src/feed/mod.rs::cleanup_removed_feed_tasks,
-- which performs the same unconditional teardown. It is fed by the feed
-- stale-deletes, which report what they removed rather than tearing down
-- inline: the DELETE statements in src/db/queries/tasks.rs carry a RETURNING
-- clause and yield src/db/mod.rs::RemovedFeedTask rows (only those still owning
-- a worktree or a tmux window — the delete is unconditional either way), which
-- reach both feed entry points on src/feed/ingest/mod.rs::FeedSyncOutcome.
-- cleanup_removed_feed_tasks groups them by repo_path and runs a repo's
-- removals sequentially — teardown_task shells git against the shared checkout,
-- so concurrent removals in one repo would contend on its index lock — while
-- distinct repos proceed in parallel. Failures are warn-logged, never
-- surfaced: feed reconciliation is background work.
--
-- How step 3 is reached, on every path. Branch deletion lives inside
-- teardown_task's worktree arm — that arm is the only thing that runs
-- `git branch -D` — so step 3 is contingent on step 2 having something to
-- remove. This is deliberate and not a per-path quirk: a task that never had a
-- worktree never had a branch either, so there is nothing to delete. Step 1 is
-- independent of both (TeardownIsOwedWheneverThereIsSomethingToRelease). Pinned
-- on the primitive by src/dispatch/tests.rs::teardown_task_kills_window_when_there_is_no_worktree
-- and teardown_task_with_neither_worktree_nor_window_runs_no_commands, and on the
-- feed wrapper by src/feed/mod.rs::cleanup_kills_window_only_when_there_is_no_worktree
-- and cleanup_of_a_stateless_row_runs_no_commands.
-- Coverage of the feed path: the src/feed/mod.rs cleanup_* tests
-- (cleanup_removes_worktree_and_kills_window,
-- cleanup_kills_window_only_when_there_is_no_worktree,
-- cleanup_of_a_stateless_row_runs_no_commands, cleanup_continues_after_a_failure,
-- cleanup_serialises_same_repo_removals), plus
-- src/feed/mod.rs::tick_removed_task_tears_down_its_worktree and
-- src/runtime/tests.rs::exec_trigger_epic_feed_removed_task_tears_down_its_worktree
-- for the wiring on each feed path.
--
-- Coverage of WorktreeIsNeverShared, in two layers.
--
-- The PREMISE — that the derived path is injective in the task id — is pinned by
-- src/dispatch/tests.rs::provision_worktree_path_is_unique_per_task_id_even_for_identical_titles.
-- That is the load-bearing test: drop the id prefix in provision_worktree and it
-- is the only one that fails.
--
-- The CONSEQUENCE — that no teardown path checks for sharing — has ONE tripwire,
-- fabricating the impossible two-rows-one-path state and asserting the teardown
-- still removes the worktree, so a reinstated guard fails the suite rather than
-- passing silently:
-- src/runtime/tests.rs::exec_cleanup_tears_down_even_if_another_row_names_the_worktree.
--
-- It sits on the archive/delete path deliberately, and there used to be a second
-- one beside the feed wrapper's own cleanup tests (a comment there records which).
-- The pair collapsed when #4096 unified both wrappers onto teardown_task: the
-- primitive takes no store handle, so a guard inside it would need a signature
-- change to be written at all, and of the two wrappers only exec_cleanup has a
-- handle in scope — it is the single place where a guard could be reintroduced
-- cheaply, so it is the place worth guarding.

rule ArchiveTask {
    when: ArchiveTask(task)

    requires: task.status != archived

    let was_running = task.status = running
    let had_worktree = task.worktree != null
    -- `released` is kept as a distinct name because WorktreeReleaseIsGated is
    -- stated in terms of release, and this binding is the single place recording
    -- that release and removal now COINCIDE: per WorktreeIsNeverShared a worktree
    -- is never shared, so it is never merely detached, and step 2 releases a
    -- worktree from this task exactly when the path is gone from disk — vacuously
    -- true when the task had no worktree.
    let removed = worktree_removed(task)
    let released = removed

    ensures:
        if task.status = done:
            task.sort_order = null
    ensures: task.status = archived
    ensures:
        if was_running:
            task.stop_pending = false
    ensures:
        if had_worktree and removed:
            not exists task.worktree
    ensures:
        if released:
            task.worktree = null
            task.tmux_window = null
        else:
            task.worktree = task.worktree
            task.tmux_window = task.tmux_window
    ensures: task.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- The resource release above is TaskTeardown (see the conceptual
        -- block at the head of this section): kill the tmux window if
        -- present; remove the git worktree if present; best-effort branch
        -- deletion. The ensures clauses above remain the normative
        -- statement of what ArchiveTask guarantees — naming the concept
        -- changed nothing about this rule's behaviour.
        --
        -- The status move is unconditional; the pointer clear is not. Per
        -- WorktreeReleaseIsGated, a task whose worktree could not be removed
        -- is still archived, but keeps its worktree and tmux pointers so the
        -- directory on disk stays reachable from the board. The failure is
        -- surfaced in the status bar AND logged at ERROR.
        --
        -- Implementation note: the removal runs in the background (it shells
        -- out to git and must not block the board), so the archive write and
        -- the pointer clear are two writes, not one — the clear is issued by
        -- the teardown's own completion path, not beside it.
        --
        -- The task.status = done check reads the pre-archive status
        -- (evaluated before the status = archived assignment below), so
        -- only a task that was actually Done has its sort_order cleared.
        -- was_running is bound in the let block for the same reason, and
        -- says so structurally: archiving a running task voids its
        -- deferred Stop (see core/Task's PendingStopOnlyWhileRunning).
        --
        -- ArchiveKeyRouting: the handler accepts any non-archived task,
        -- but the TUI's 'x' key only routes *Done* tasks here. On a task
        -- in any other status 'x' moves it straight to Done through
        -- ConfirmDone instead, since completing is the common case and
        -- archiving the exception. Concretely, for the 'x' key:
        --   * single task in Done -> ArchiveTask confirmation
        --   * single task in any other status -> ConfirmDone
        --   * multi-selection of tasks only, all in Done -> BatchArchive
        --   * multi-selection of tasks only, at least one not in Done ->
        --     ConfirmDone over the non-Done tasks; the already-Done ones
        --     in the selection are left untouched
        --   * any selection containing an epic, and a single selected
        --     epic -> BatchArchive / ArchiveEpic as before
        -- 'x' pressed in the archive panel is unrelated: it deletes (see
        -- DeleteTask).
}

rule BatchArchive {
    when: BatchArchive(tasks, epics)

    -- Archives selected tasks and epics in one operation.
    -- Tasks are archived unconditionally (same as ArchiveTask).
    -- Epics with non-done active subtasks are skipped.

    ensures:
        for task in tasks:
            ArchiveTask(task)
    ensures:
        for epic in epics:
            if epic.active_subtasks.all(t => t.status = done)
               or epic.active_subtasks.count = 0:
                ArchiveEpic(epic)

    @guidance
        -- Triggered from the TUI (x key) when epics are multi-selected,
        -- or when the multi-selection is tasks that are all already in
        -- Done — otherwise 'x' routes to ConfirmDone (see the
        -- ArchiveKeyRouting guidance on ArchiveTask). Shows a count-based
        -- confirmation prompt before proceeding. Skipped epics (those with
        -- non-done subtasks) are reported in the status bar.
}

rule DeleteTask {
    when: DeleteTask(task)

    requires: task.status = archived

    let had_worktree = task.worktree != null
    -- See ArchiveTask: removal from disk is the only way to be released, since
    -- WorktreeIsNeverShared rules out a merely-detached worktree.
    let removed = worktree_removed(task)
    let released = removed

    ensures:
        if had_worktree and removed:
            not exists task.worktree
    ensures:
        if released:
            not exists task
        else:
            task.status = archived
            task.worktree = task.worktree
            task.tmux_window = task.tmux_window

    @guidance
        -- Permanent removal. Only accessible from the archive view.
        --
        -- The delete is GATED on the teardown (WorktreeReleaseIsGated at the
        -- head of this section): if the worktree could not be removed, the row
        -- is NOT deleted. The task stays archived, keeps its worktree and tmux
        -- pointers, the failure is surfaced and logged at ERROR, and pressing
        -- delete again retries the removal. A task with no worktree has nothing
        -- to release, so it is deleted whatever the teardown reports — a
        -- window-only row still gets its tmux window killed first (step 1 of
        -- TaskTeardown), and a failure there is warn-logged without withholding
        -- the delete. A row owning neither worktree nor window is deleted
        -- immediately, with no teardown at all.
        -- The resource release above is TaskTeardown (see the conceptual
        -- block at the head of this section) — the same teardown
        -- ArchiveTask performs: tmux window killed, worktree removed, and
        -- best-effort branch deletion. The ensures clauses above remain
        -- the normative statement of what DeleteTask guarantees; naming
        -- the concept changed nothing about this rule's behaviour.
        -- The archived-status precondition is enforced by the TUI input
        -- layer (archive view only shows archived tasks), not by the
        -- handler itself.
        --
        -- Archive navigation: the archive view is a hidden column to the
        -- right of Done. Press l from the Done column to reveal it; press
        -- h, Left, or Esc to return to Done. The archive is not shown as
        -- a regular kanban column — it appears as an overlay panel when
        -- the virtual archive column (index 4) is selected.
}

-- == Copy Task ==

rule CopyTask {
    when: CopyTask(source)

    ensures: core/Task.created(
        title: "Copy of: " + source.title,
        description: source.description,
        repo_path: confirmed_repo_path,  -- user confirms or edits before creation
        status: backlog,
        sub_status: core/SubStatus.default_for(backlog),
        tag: source.tag,
        wrap_up_mode: source.wrap_up_mode,
        created_at: now,
        updated_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- Copies a subset of fields from the source task, including the
        -- wrap_up_mode preference. Fields that are NOT copied: plan,
        -- worktree, tmux_window, url, epic, sort_order, base_branch.
        -- auto_run_plan is NOT copied — like plan_path, it's tied to a
        -- specific (unshared) plan file, and this field is set by skills
        -- (decompose-review) rather than being a human TUI preference.
        -- The new task is a clean backlog item with no agent history.
        --
        -- schedule_interval_secs and pinned_branch are NOT copied either.
        -- Both are properties of one scheduled job, not preferences: two
        -- tasks polling the same pinned branch on the same cadence would
        -- double-dispatch it, so the copy starts unscheduled and the copy
        -- flow's own schedule gate (below) is where the user opts back in.
        --
        -- The copy flow runs the tail of the manual creation form:
        -- InputRepoPath -> InputBaseBranch -> InputWrapUpMode -> the schedule
        -- gate (see CreateTask's guidance; the copy pays the same single
        -- keypress, and reaches the two text steps only via "s"). The user is
        -- prompted to confirm or edit the repo_path before the task is
        -- created (the source's repo_path is pre-filled as default).
        -- base_branch is NOT copied from the source task and is NOT taken
        -- from config.default_branch either: the flow re-prompts through the
        -- same BaseBranchPicker surface as task creation (see
        -- dispatch.allium), prefilled with the most-recently-used branch
        -- for the confirmed repo_path and falling back to
        -- config.default_branch when that repo has no history yet.
        -- The source's wrap_up_mode seeds the draft, but the
        -- InputWrapUpMode prompt that follows records whatever the user
        -- answers there (Enter = no preference), so the copied value only
        -- survives if the user re-picks it.
}

-- == Editor ==

rule EditTask {
    when: EditTask(task)

    -- Opens the user's editor — resolved by the shared rule stated with
    -- core/config.editor_fallback (VISUAL, else EDITOR, else that fallback;
    -- absent/empty/whitespace counts as unset; the value is split into argv and
    -- run with no shell, so `EDITOR="vim -p"` works) — with a
    -- structured file containing the task's
    -- title, description, repo_path, status, plan, tag, base_branch,
    -- wrap_up_mode, url, url_type, schedule_interval_secs and pinned_branch.
    -- The sections are delimited by `--- SECTION ---` markers
    -- (TITLE, DESCRIPTION, REPO_PATH, STATUS, PLAN, TAG, BASE_BRANCH,
    -- WRAP_UP_MODE, URL, URL_TYPE, SCHEDULE_INTERVAL_SECS, PINNED_BRANCH).
    -- The user edits the file; on save, fields are parsed back.
    -- Empty title/description/repo_path retain their previous values.
    -- Empty plan or tag clears the field. Empty base_branch retains the
    -- previous value. Empty wrap_up_mode clears the preference.
    -- Empty SCHEDULE_INTERVAL_SECS unschedules the task; empty PINNED_BRANCH
    -- unpins it. Emptying a section is how each is turned off, the same
    -- clear-on-empty convention EditEpic's feed sections use -- and the only
    -- way to turn scheduling off from the editor, since there is no "off"
    -- keyword.
    --
    -- SCHEDULE_INTERVAL_SECS accepts the shared interval-literal grammar
    -- (core.allium: "How a human types an interval"), so "10m" and "600" are
    -- the same edit. The section is WRITTEN back as bare seconds, so a
    -- suffixed value the user typed reappears unsuffixed on the next open;
    -- that is intended, not a round-trip bug -- seconds are what the column
    -- holds, and re-rendering the user's spelling would mean storing it.
    -- An unparseable value follows the same rule as the enum-ish sections
    -- below: no value, a recorded parse error, and -- because "no value"
    -- means clear for this field -- the task is UNSCHEDULED. This is the
    -- sharp edge of the clear-on-empty convention: a typo in the interval
    -- silently stops the schedule rather than preserving it. It is accepted
    -- for consistency with FEED_INTERVAL_SECS, which has always behaved this
    -- way, and it is not silent in practice -- the parse error is surfaced as
    -- a status message and the card's scheduled badge disappears.
    --
    -- PINNED_BRANCH is free text taken literally, with no existence check
    -- (see CreateTask's schedule-step guidance for why).
    --
    -- The enum-ish sections (STATUS, TAG, WRAP_UP_MODE, URL_TYPE) all share
    -- one parse rule: an empty section and an unparseable section are
    -- treated identically — the section yields no value. Unparseable input
    -- additionally records a parse error, and all accumulated errors are
    -- surfaced together as a status message ("Edit accepted with parse
    -- errors — ..."); the edit is still applied. What "no value" means is
    -- per-field: STATUS retains the previous status, TAG and WRAP_UP_MODE
    -- clear the field, and URL_TYPE falls back as described below.
    --
    -- url is computed from the URL and URL_TYPE sections, then diffed
    -- against the task's prior url; the field is only touched when it
    -- changes (an unchanged edit is a no-op for url):
    --   - URL empty -> the url is cleared (set to none).
    --   - URL non-empty with a valid URL_TYPE -> url set to
    --     {url, that type}.
    --   - URL non-empty with empty URL_TYPE -> if the url string is
    --     unchanged from the prior value, the prior url_type is
    --     preserved (so e.g. a security_alert is not silently
    --     downgraded); otherwise the type is inferred from the url
    --     string (".../pull/..." -> pr, ".../issues/..." -> issue,
    --     else other).
    --   - URL non-empty with an unparseable URL_TYPE -> a parse error
    --     is recorded (surfaced as a status message) and the same
    --     empty-URL_TYPE fallback applies (preserve prior type if the
    --     url string is unchanged, else infer from the string).

    let prior = task.status

    ensures:
        let edited = EditorSession(task)
        if edited.title != "": task.title = edited.title
        if edited.description != "": task.description = edited.description
        if edited.repo_path != "": task.repo_path = edited.repo_path
        if edited.status != null:
            task.status = edited.status
            task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(edited.status)
            -- Entering/leaving done via the STATUS field carries the same
            -- completion-recency sort_order side effect as ConfirmDone /
            -- MoveTaskBackward, and UpdateEpicViaMcp's mirror of it on the
            -- epic side (epics.allium): the editor is a freeform status
            -- setter, but it still routes through update_task, which does
            -- not special-case "how" the status changed.
            if edited.status = done and prior != done:
                task.sort_order = completion_recency_rank(now)
            else if prior = done and edited.status != done:
                task.sort_order = null
            -- And the same leaving-running clear, for the same reason: the
            -- editor routes through update_task, which applies every rule a
            -- transition derives from the prior status. See core/Task's
            -- PendingStopOnlyWhileRunning invariant.
            if prior = running and edited.status != running:
                task.stop_pending = false
        if edited.plan != "": task.plan_path = edited.plan
        else: task.plan_path = null
        if edited.tag != "": task.tag = edited.tag
        else: task.tag = null
        if edited.base_branch != "": task.base_branch = edited.base_branch
        task.wrap_up_mode = edited.wrap_up_mode
        task.schedule_interval_secs = edited.schedule_interval_secs
        if edited.pinned_branch != "": task.pinned_branch = edited.pinned_branch
        else: task.pinned_branch = null
        -- url updated from the URL/URL_TYPE sections per the semantics
        -- in the comment above; left untouched when unchanged.
        let desired_url =
            if edited.url = "": null
            else if edited.url_type != null: core/TaskUrl{url: edited.url, url_type: edited.url_type}
            else if task.url != null and task.url.url = edited.url:
                core/TaskUrl{url: edited.url, url_type: task.url.url_type}
            else: core/TaskUrl{url: edited.url, url_type: infer_url_type(edited.url)}
        if desired_url != task.url: task.url = desired_url
        task.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Status changes via the editor bypass normal transition
        -- preconditions. The user can set any valid TaskStatus
        -- regardless of the current status or the transitions
        -- declared on Task. This is intentional: the editor is a
        -- power-user escape hatch for manual corrections, and
        -- core.allium's Task.status graph records it as exempt.
        -- The exemption includes `archived`, which the graph marks
        -- terminal: the archive view binds the same editor key, so an
        -- archived task can be edited back to backlog, running, review
        -- or done.
        -- Sub_status is auto-reset to the default for the new status
        -- when status changes, per the SubStatusAutoreset invariant.
        -- The editor opens in a separate tmux window; tmux focus
        -- switches to that window while the TUI keeps running in
        -- its original window. On editor exit the window closes and
        -- focus returns to the TUI window. At most one editor
        -- session is live at a time — a second EditTask while an
        -- editor is already open is a no-op with a status message.
        -- A confirmation prompt (ConfirmEditTask) is shown before
        -- the editor is invoked.
        -- The sort_order side effect mirrors ConfirmDone's entering-done
        -- set and MoveTaskBackward's leaving-done clear (both in this
        -- file), and UpdateEpicViaMcp's entering/leaving-done clause in
        -- epics.allium. This was a spec-only gap: the code already
        -- implements and tests it (sort_order_for_status_transition, run
        -- inside TaskService::update_task; see
        -- update_task_archived_to_backlog_is_unaffected_by_done_rule in
        -- src/service/tasks/tests.rs).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- DoneRequiresHumanConfirmation: The Done status transition requires
-- explicit human confirmation via the TUI. MCP tools cannot set status
-- to done or archived. This is enforced in the McpUpdateTask rule
-- preconditions and the ConfirmDone rule requiring review status.
-- Exception: PrMerged transitions are automatic because the PR merge on
-- GitHub constitutes human confirmation via the code review process.
-- WrapUpRebase is agent-initiated (the MCP wrap_up tool); its Done
-- transition is applied by the agent's subsequent exit_session call.

-- DispatchMcpToolsAreTransparent: Dispatch MCP tool calls (get_task,
-- wrap_up, update_task, etc.) must not trigger automatic status
-- transitions. They are management operations, not implementation work.
-- The PreToolUse hook filters them out before `dispatch hook` is even
-- called. Non-MCP tools (Bash, Read, etc.) do reach the hook, but they
-- cannot move a review task either: HookPreToolUse is gated to tasks
-- already in running, so a review task's PreToolUse is a no-op (see
-- agent-health.allium). Review -> Running happens only via
-- HookUserPromptSubmit, i.e. when the human sends a new prompt. The
-- wrap_up handler accepts any running or review task with a worktree.

-- SubStatusConsistency: A task's sub_status must always be valid for
-- its parent status: sub_status.is_valid_for(status) = true. This is
-- enforced at every status transition: when status changes, sub_status
-- is reset to SubStatus.default_for(new_status) before being persisted.
-- Individual handlers may then set a more specific sub_status (e.g.,
-- DetectStaleAgent sets stale, HookNotification sets needs_input) only
-- after the reset.

-- SubStatusAutoreset: Whenever task.status changes, task.sub_status is
-- reset to SubStatus.default_for(new_status). This ensures no invalid
-- (status, sub_status) combinations are ever persisted.
-- This is a single centrally-enforced invariant, not an obligation on each
-- individual rule: the write path derives the effective sub_status for
-- every task write as "the explicitly supplied sub_status, else
-- SubStatus.default_for(the supplied status)". A rule that changes status
-- without mentioning sub_status therefore gets the reset for free, and the
-- sub_status assertions written out in the rules above document the
-- resulting state rather than adding a per-rule duty. A write supplying an
-- explicit (status, sub_status) pair that is invalid is rejected before it
-- reaches the store, and a database constraint backs the invariant up.
-- Exception: writes that set a specific sub_status override the default
-- (e.g., HookNotification keeps status=running and sets
-- sub_status=needs_input; FinishTaskConflict keeps the parent status and
-- sets sub_status=conflict).

